title: Counting down
mode: draw
chapter: Counting
check: behaves
parts: dff not chip:my_xor
needs: my_xor
---- intro
Count backwards: 3, 2, 1, 0, then round to 3. Watch the bits:

| count | q1 | q0 |
|-------|----|----|
| 3 | 1 | 1 |
| 2 | 1 | 0 |
| 1 | 0 | 1 |
| 0 | 0 | 0 |
| 3 | 1 | 1 |

- `q0` still flips at every tick.
- `q1` now flips when `q0` is **0**: the new `q1` is `q1` XOR (NOT `q0`).

It starts at 0, so the first tick takes it to 3.
---- task
Make `q1 q0` count 0, 3, 2, 1, 0 ... one step down per tick.
---- start
clock clk 0 220
led q0 560 80
led q1 560 220
---- reference
clock clk
led q0, q1
wire a[1], b[1]
a = dff(~a, clk)
b = dff(b ^ ~a, clk)
q0 = a
q1 = b
---- answer
clock clk 0 220
not n0 160 0
dff f0 160 80
not n1 260 140
chip x1 my_xor 300 220
dff f1 440 220
led q0 560 80
led q1 560 220
clk -> f0.clk
f0.q -> n0.in
n0 -> f0.d
f0.q -> q0
f0.q -> n1.in
f1.q -> x1.a
n1 -> x1.b
x1.y -> f1.d
clk -> f1.clk
f1.q -> q1
---- hint1
`q0` is the up counter's `q0`: a NOT from its `q` back to its `d`.
---- hint2
For `q1`, my_xor gets `q1` and NOT `q0` (a second NOT gate), and feeds the second flip-flop's `d`.
---- solution
Flip-flop 0 flips every tick. Flip-flop 1 takes `q1` XOR NOT `q0`, so it flips when `q0` is 0. Step: 0, 3, 2, 1, 0.
---- done
One NOT gate turns an up counter into a down counter. Timers that count down to zero, like a microwave's, work like this.
